WEBVTT

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:08.150
3) Go in for the score 
2) If possible, take a shot 
1) Observe

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:08.150
2) Go in for the score
1) If possible, take a shot

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.950
<i>I'll use the two-phase method</i>

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.150
<i>I learned in Florida to go for the win 
from the baseline when possible.</i>

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:13.040
<i>I'll win points by increasing 
the risk of those shots</i>

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.240
<i>to the utmost limit by changing the pace.</i>

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.010
<i>I'll increase my ball speed overall,</i>

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:24.220
<i>but when I hit a slower ball, I'll control 
it with pinpoint accuracy as best I can.</i>

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:28.880
<i>This is close to the ultimate 
tennis that I strive for.</i>

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.800
<i>Since I'm not scoring aces 
during my service games now,</i>

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.720
<i>I have to change something or 
I won't be able to hold serve.</i>

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.630
<i>I expected to make more mistakes in this game.</i>

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.720
<i>But for me to take this set,</i>

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.510
<i>I have to attack boldly!</i>

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.150
<i>I got it to the corner, 
but it came right back at me!</i>

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.510
<i>Now I'll just barely keep it straight, and...</i>

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.380
<i>add spin!</i>

00:00:58.610 --> 00:00:59.990
Love-15.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:38.260
Recklessness and Surprise

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.760
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.830
<i>It's not just my flat shots.</i>

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.930
<i>He's trying to score on my spin shots, too?!</i>

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.050
<i>He aims for tight areas without hesitation,</i>

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.320
<i>just like when I faced him last year.</i>

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.570
<i>But his technique's improved remarkably.</i>

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.930
<i>Does this mean he'll try to score on 
all my shots that aren't slices?</i>

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:07.080
<i>No, that's too risky even for Nabae-kun.</i>

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.650
<i>He only went after it because 
he didn't want to lose the set,</i>

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.900
<i>and he was prepared to miss.</i>

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.610
<i>This may be the first time I've known 
Nabae-kun to take such a risk.</i>

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.570
<i>He's playing his strongest cards 
first, regardless of the risk.</i>

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:23.510
<i>Which means even if I take risks too, 
I probably can't beat him.</i>

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:24.430
<i>Then...</i>

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.000
For example, you know...

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.220
<i>I'm the challenger.</i>

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.770
<i>All or nothing!</i>

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:34.940
15-all.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.400
<i>A quick serve?</i>

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.310
Huh? What was that?

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:41.310
The ball was slow, but the timing was quick.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.160
That kind of trick move is possible?

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:48.170
<i>Wow, I didn't expect him to try the 
serve he just learned last night.</i>

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.790
<i>He's really giving it his all.</i>

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.310
<i>I-I did it. It went in!</i>

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.790
<i>And I got the ace!</i>

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.780
<i>It wasn't an incredible serve,</i>

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.600
<i>but it worked because Nabae-kun doesn't 
have any data on my quick serves.</i>

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.220
<i>Because I did something I normally wouldn't!</i>

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.440
<i>This unpredictable element may be 
a strength of Maruo-kun's, as well.</i>

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.060
<i>But I won't fall for that one again.</i>

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.440
<i>Next, I'll take advantage of that slow serve.</i>

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.120
<i>Even if that means lowering my desired 
70% likelihood of getting the first serve in,</i>

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.780
<i>I'll drastically increase 
the speed to make up for it.</i>

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:23.070
<i>I need my best flat serve right now!</i>

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:27.720
<i>What?!</i>

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.330
15-30.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.030
A-Amazing!

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:33.410
What the hell was that?!

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.680
He stepped up ahead of that super-fast 
flat serve and shot it back!

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.250
H-He's on another level!

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:40.670
<i>No way.</i>

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.720
<i>He predicted my pattern?</i>

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.940
<i>Even if he did, this hasn't 
happened since Ike-kun.</i>

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.550
<i>He's that strong?</i>

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.340
<i>Yeah, he is.</i>

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.580
<i>Nabae-kun plays smart tennis, 
changing styles with each opponent,</i>

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.560
<i>so I just didn't realize how good he really was.</i>

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.210
<i>He continues to reign as Japan's best player.</i>

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.190
<i>This is the true ability of Japan's best.</i>

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:06.830
<i>I have to defeat him.</i>

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:10.420
<i>No, that's exactly why I want to beat him!</i>

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.950
<i>So for now, I'm going to do everything I can!</i>

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.590
<i>What can I do against Japan's best player,</i>

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.050
<i>who takes such risks to come after me?</i>

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.090
<i>I'm only three points away from 
taking back the first set.</i>

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.910
<i>If it's just three points, it won't 
be easy, but I have a chance!</i>

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.850
<i>To do that, I have to surpass his 
expectations, like that quick serve earlier.</i>

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.610
<i>For example, I could forget 
about my serve accuracy</i>

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.360
<i>and go for a serve at max speed 
that just barely stays in.</i>

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.860
Center area     Wide area

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.520
<i>I'd use a flat serve with 
as little spin as possible</i>

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.860
<i>to aim for either wide or center.</i>

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:48.840
<i>In other words, I'd be aiming for just 
about a ball's height above the net,</i>

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.620
<i>either there... or there.</i>

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:52.740
<i>Put all my body's strength into the delivery!</i>

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.370
<i>He's really pushing hard for an ace.</i>

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.890
<i>I have to get the next one in.</i>

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.130
<i>But that's exactly why...</i>

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.260
<i>I'll use something I normally wouldn't...</i>

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.010
30-all.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.690
What was that?!

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:12.000
That was insanely fast!

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.530
It's good that it went in, 
but that was the second serve!

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.270
<i>I-It went in!</i>

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.900
<i>He's being way too reckless.</i>

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.790
<i>I think I was able to turn the pressure 
into power on that second serve.</i>

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.780
<i>But I can't do reckless things 
like that too many times.</i>

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.020
<i>Next point!</i>

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.780
<i>The speed dropped,
but the placement is good.</i>

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.610
<i>Now give this one all I've got!</i>

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.840
<i>While my serve gives me the upper hand,</i>

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.580
<i>I'll try a power battle I don't do often.</i>

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.010
<i>I won't change the pace much, 
but quicken the tempo with fast shots.</i>

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.010
<i>Don't think about dividing up the court,</i>

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.510
<i>but just aim toward open court!</i>

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:54.890
<i>It's gonna come back!</i>

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.150
40-30.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:00.690
Yes!

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:02.750
He pushed for it and scored!

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:03.590
Seriously?!

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:06.050
Maruo's finally got set point!

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:10.400
You're amazing, Bro!

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.370
<i>After all this, he's going for a 
preemptive attack I have no data on.</i>

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:17.290
<i>But this isn't how Maruo-kun truly plays.</i>

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:22.170
Maruo Eiichiro

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:22.170
Mental Fortitude
Decision Making
Strategic Sense

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.460
<i>This strategy abandons his style.</i>

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.170
<i>It's more reckless than it is unpredictable.</i>

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.000
<i>I'll go in for the score here.</i>

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.920
<i>It would be next to impossible 
to break Nabae-kun's serve now.</i>

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.640
<i>So I can't let this opportunity slip away.</i>

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.220
<i>This first serve is absolutely crucial!</i>

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.550
<i>Consider the speed, placement, 
and probability equally!</i>

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.450
<i>I'll go for a spin that bounces outwards.</i>

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.020
<i>Give it an angle and go deep!</i>

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.520
<i>He's back to changing the pace again?</i>

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.230
<i>Now a rising shot, even if I have to force it.</i>

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:51.350
<i>A rising shot, </i>and<i> he's moving up?</i>

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.990
<i>This shot isn't that easy.</i>

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.360
<i>And drop!</i>

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:56.490
<i>Was it too high?</i>

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.120
<i>And now this?</i>

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:00.860
<i>Even if he gets to it, it just 
needs to break his balance.</i>

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.170
<i>If I can get close now, it'll be a volley battle!</i>

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:05.630
<i>The one thing I'm confident 
I can beat Nabae-kun in!</i>

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:07.290
<i>Crosscourt!</i>

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.630
<i>No! It's down the line?!</i>

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:16.960
Out.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.930
First set winner: Maruo. Score: 6-4.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.520
Seriously?

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.860
Maruo stayed on top!

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:26.730
Nabae's down a set for the 
first time in this tournament!

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.350
<i>I won the set!</i>

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:31.570
<i>I took the first set from Nabae-kun!</i>

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:36.540
<i>But he completely saw through that last play.</i>

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.340
<i>This isn't the time to be celebrating.</i>

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:40.010
<i>A surprise attack just happened to work.</i>

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:42.580
<i>I can't continue using this strategy.</i>

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.600
This is the first time I've ever 
seen Nabae down a set.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.710
Me, too. I can't wait to see where this goes.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:51.880
<i>That punk really did it.</i>

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.090
A kid who's played tennis
for only three years

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:55.970
was able to take a set from Nabae-kun.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:58.700
It must be a result of 
your coaching, Aoi-kun.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.660
Oh, Miyamoto-san.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.600
Nope, that's all him.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:07.720
If that's true, he might really 
be something special.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.900
Maybe this means there's 
a chance for Maruo-kun, too.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:13.960
It's hard to say anything yet.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:18.040
But Nabae-kun has a degree of composure 
that seems unreal for a junior athlete,

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:21.660
so I was hoping to put pressure on him 
as early in the match as possible.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.240
If I'm not mistaken, Nabae-kun got this strong

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:29.040
after he learned to keep that 
composure in any situation.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.330
I'd say there aren't even many
professional athletes who

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:35.050
can keep their emotions 
controlled as well as he can.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.450
Takuma-san did mention before

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:41.200
that when he was younger, 
he lost his temper more than Araya.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:47.440
That's right. I never expected 
Nabae-kun to mature this much.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.350
Hey, that's Nabae, right?

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:56.690
He's a lot stronger than he looks.

00:09:56.690 --> 00:10:00.300
No, that's what flawlessly efficient 
tennis is supposed to look like.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.580
His stability is unparalleled.

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.320
But he won't be able to win today.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:10.330
<i>He's so strong.</i>

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:13.110
<i>Even though he started years after I did...</i>

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:16.210
Ike Soji
Nabae Yu

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:21.980
<i>He defies all logic.</i>

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:23.380
Game, set, and match.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.850
Winner: Ike. Score: 6-1, 6-0.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:27.890
Yes!

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:37.860
You were so affected that you 
damaged your precious racket?

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.810
No one can completely 
suppress their emotions.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.520
No matter how strong your will may be,

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.370
there are times when you can't keep it in.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:50.340
You should accept that and come 
up with a way to deal with it.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.840
There has to be a way to minimize 
the opportunity for emotions

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.760
to make their way onto the court.

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:05.890
<i>He really got me on those last three points.</i>

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.190
<i>When I made Maruo-kun choose strategies</i>

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.300
<i>he normally wouldn't, like the reckless 
second serve and the power battle,</i>

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:17.570
<i>the match was moving in my favor, 
even though he won the points.</i>

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:23.280
<i>But with that drop shot he used at the end 
to take it into a volley battle, his specialty,</i>

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.150
<i>I should've played it safe and aimed for 
his feet so I could gain the advantage,</i>

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.510
<i>and gone for the winning hit afterwards.</i>

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:30.460
<i>And yet...</i>

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.420
<i>The moment I remembered losing 
last year's volley battle with him,</i>

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.340
<i>I wanted to win so badly that 
I lost control of my emotions.</i>

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:41.430
Time.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:45.790
<i>That's right.</i>

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:48.180
<i>Calmly analyze yourself.</i>

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:54.660
First of all, stop trying to process 
emotions you can't deal with.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.320
If you were alone, you wouldn't 
be swayed by emotions.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.770
Since the emotions you have
are caused by your opponent,

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:03.950
shouldn't you make use of your opponent?

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:10.030
Imagine it as best you can during a point.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.710
Put all of the strong emotions

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:14.840
that your opponent causes into the ball.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:36.650
<i>Put all of the strong emotions that 
your opponent causes into the ball,</i>

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:39.020
<i>and send it back to the center 
of your opponent's court.</i>

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:41.950
<i>The ball went toward the center.</i>

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:43.030
<i>Is this...</i>

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:47.410
<i>Your opponent will undoubtedly try 
to hit that ball with all his might.</i>

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:52.050
<i>The ball that holds all your emotions</i>

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:53.790
<i>will disappear from the court,</i>

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.190
<i>in exchange for sacrificing a point.</i>

00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:59.040
Love-15.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.660
<i>This sensation...</i>

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.070
<i>It happened last year, too.</i>

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:10.260
<i>After that, Nabae-kun gave me 
a show of his true strength.</i>

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:17.940
<i>This is probably the method
Nabae-kun uses to switch gears.</i>

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:20.790
<i>Araya-kun had one, too.</i>

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:26.200
<i>Now that I'm aware of it, 
I won't let it work for him.</i>

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:30.680
<i>All right.</i>

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:38.220
15-all.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.320
<i>Amazing...</i>

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:41.590
<i>What a sharp slice serve.</i>

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:46.900
<i>I can't let him have the 
upper hand during his serve.</i>

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:48.640
<i>Time for a deep, heavy stroke.</i>

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.890
<i>And he finishes with a shot that easily 
targets the edges of my defense.</i>

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.820
<i>He can play like this because 
he has such minute data on me.</i>

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.230
<i>Nabae-kun's method allows him</i>

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.820
<i>to efficiently alter his play 
depending on the opponent.</i>

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:10.130
<i>Against a ten, he goes for eleven. 
Against an eleven, he goes for twelve.</i>

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:12.080
<i>It's a play style that minimizes risk.</i>

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:16.180
<i>This can be done because he comprehends 
and calculates everything about me.</i>

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:20.080
<i>It's flawless tennis that combines 
decisiveness and stability.</i>

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:23.880
Game: Nabae. 1-0.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.170
Second set.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:29.290
<i>How will Nabae-kun come at 
me in my service game?</i>

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:31.180
<i>There's no recovering if he takes a set.</i>

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:34.180
<i>I want to avoid the pressure 
of having a close match.</i>

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:35.760
<i>That being said,</i>

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.580
<i>it's likely that he would take the risk 
and go for a winner right off the bat.</i>

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.190
<i>This is the critical moment.</i>

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:45.560
<i>I somehow got through the first 
set by maneuvering around,</i>

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.450
<i>but that kind of tactic won't work forever.</i>

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:52.740
<i>But this is also my time to take 
risks, since I'm ahead by a set.</i>

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:56.580
<i>I'll use my 1/81 control to set 
up my change-of-pace strategy.</i>

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.210
<i>I'll make use of all the data I've collected,</i>

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.140
<i>and meet him head-on to keep my lead!</i>

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:07.010
<i>I knew it. He really is going for it.</i>

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:08.880
Love-15.

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:13.760
<i>He's aiming for my flat and spin shots, 
so I can't allow any weak hits.</i>

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:17.640
<i>That being said, I can't 
depend on indecisive slices.</i>

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:21.180
<i>The pace changes each time, so if it 
turns into a ground stroke battle,</i>

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:22.840
<i>I'll have to limit my target.</i>

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:27.530
<i>But as of now, there isn't 
much variation in change.</i>

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.530
<i>I'll take my chances here, too!</i>

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.530
15-all.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:33.820
Yes!

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:36.790
<i>If I push it this far, I can still win points!</i>

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:39.660
15-30.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.580
<i>This is why you're forced 
into unreasonable shots.</i>

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.920
<i>It's clear after playing to this point.</i>

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:51.930
<i>Because you're a lot like me.</i>

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:54.930
15-40.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.190
<i>So Maruo-kun should understand</i>

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.630
<i>that I have to give it my all here.</i>

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.410
Game: Nabae. 2-0.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:04.510
Second set.

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:05.840
Nabae broke serve!

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:09.860
Are you kidding? He's overpowering him
just as the second set's getting started!

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:13.320
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:16:11.950 --> 00:16:14.370
Bro's starting to struggle.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.710
Looks like Nabae switched 
gears into winning mode.

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:20.330
He's got his composure back in full.

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:23.580
Now there's no opportunity 
to take advantage of.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.500
<i>I was finally able to hold serve for one game.</i>

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.840
<i>But Nabae-kun hasn't done 
anything risky to corner me yet.</i>

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:33.800
<i>If I can just hold serve again, 
I can take this set.</i>

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:38.470
<i>So that one game was the real Nabae-kun.</i>

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:42.310
<i>But I can't envision myself 
beating him right now.</i>

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:46.500
<i>My change-of-pace isn't a strategy 
that can go toe-to-toe with that.</i>

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:50.190
<i>But does that mean I can't win this match?</i>

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:53.320
<i>Something... There has to be something.</i>

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:55.670
<i>Think... Think of something!</i>

00:16:57.450 --> 00:16:58.780
There is!

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:00.950
There's just one way: getting in the zone.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:06.130
<i>If I'm close to being in the zone 
the way I was in the first set,</i>

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.620
<i>do I have a chance?</i>

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:15.930
Anxiety                    Exhaustion
Ability
Pressure                    Adversity

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:12.010
In the sports world, athletes at the
highest level are said to have

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:15.930
the ability to create that state of mind at will.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:21.680
<i>Aoi-san said there are those who can 
"create that state of mind at will."</i>

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:32.940
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:26.300
<i>I've won a set, so the current score 
makes it easy to focus on the now.</i>

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:29.110
<i>I can still take some reckless actions,</i>

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:32.940
<i>in order to get from where I am 
now to being close to the zone.</i>

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:36.230
<i>Is there any useful data for that?</i>

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:40.200
<i>There have been only four times I've 
been close to being in the zone.</i>

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.450
<i>The second half of my match with Alex.</i>

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:47.210
<i>The match with Araya-kun, 
after becoming exhausted.</i>

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.290
<i>The final moments in my match with Ide-kun.</i>

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:56.590
<i>And the beginning of this match.</i>

00:17:56.900 --> 00:18:00.400
<i>I know that being in the zone means you 
can focus only on the here and now.</i>

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:04.080
<i>But other than that, what do those 
four situations have in common?</i>

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.500
<i>Was it that all the opponents were 
strong, and I felt it was a challenge?</i>

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.600
<i>That can't be the only thing.</i>

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:10.860
Time.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:13.000
<i>What's certain is that</i>

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:17.430
<i>gathering enough data made me 
feel less doubt about what to do,</i>

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.300
<i>and my instincts took priority.</i>

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:25.160
<i>I have Nabae-kun's data from 
today in my notes and in my head.</i>

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:28.120
<i>Just trust that my instincts will 
know the best course of action...</i>

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:31.380
<i>There!</i>

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:34.900
<i>No! I was consciously thinking just now!</i>

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:40.260
15-love.

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:41.660
<i>All right.</i>

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:44.040
<i>I won the point, so it's not that bad.</i>

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:47.000
<i>But this is just being able 
to play with all my strength.</i>

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.020
<i>It's not the zone.</i>

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:54.310
<i>It's not the light feeling I had earlier,
when my feet seemed to move on their own.</i>

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:57.130
Game: Maruo. 4-2.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:59.070
Nabae leads. Second set.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:01.780
<i>But, somehow, I'm able to hold serve.</i>

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.930
<i>Nabae-kun isn't pushing for the break right now,</i>

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:06.700
<i>so the problem is what comes next.</i>

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.080
<i>If I don't break at some point, I'll lose the set.</i>

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:16.400
Game: Nabae. 5-2.

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:17.580
Second set.

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:20.220
<i>But I can't break!</i>

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:24.130
<i>If he takes another game, I'll lose the set.</i>

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:26.150
<i>If we go into the final set like this,</i>

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:28.470
<i>it'll unfold just like the last time.</i>

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:31.460
<i>Is there anything that can help me hold out?</i>

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:34.040
<i>Anything that can bring me closer to the zone?</i>

00:19:36.370 --> 00:19:37.600
<i>There's one more!</i>

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.860
<i>Another commonality of 
the times I was in the zone!</i>

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:45.030
<i>All four times, I didn't get 
into the zone by trying to.</i>

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:46.870
<i>It happened subconsciously!</i>

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:50.970
<i>Is that a factor for getting into the zone?</i>

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:53.550
<i>So do athletes that can willingly 
get themselves there</i>

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.640
<i>have the ability to consciously 
control an unconscious condition?</i>

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:58.250
<i>Consciously unconscious...</i>

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:01.130
<i>What does that even mean?</i>

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:02.630
Time.

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:04.990
<i>I wanted a little more time.</i>

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:07.460
<i>Should I stop thinking about the zone for now?</i>

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:10.310
<i>No, then I'd just be right where
I've been all this time.</i>

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:13.060
<i>I'll just have to do something 
about it while playing.</i>

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:17.420
<i>I was able to hold serve, so I'll 
look for a way during this game.</i>

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:19.280
<i>I'll prioritize my instincts,</i>

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:22.700
<i>but hold serve while trying 
to find a path to the zone!</i>

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:28.460
Love-15.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:30.570
<i>It's no good.</i>

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:34.060
<i>I can't rely on my instincts 
if I'm thinking so much.</i>

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:35.710
<i>Just focus on holding serve here.</i>

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:41.210
<i>His aggressiveness vanished from his play.</i>

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:47.200
Love-30.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:48.970
<i>His movements are hesitant.</i>

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:52.120
The pace is completely one-sided. 
It's not like Maruo.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:55.140
That's what happens when 
you're on the defensive.

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:58.650
Bro... Please do something about this!

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:04.700
15-30.

00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:07.450
15-40.

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:09.840
<i>Damn, isn't there something?!</i>

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:11.550
Here we go, set point!

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:13.830
Nabae, hit a winner and take it all home!

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:15.850
<i>Something!</i>

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:29.620
Out.

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:31.770
Second set winner: Nabae.

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:33.500
Score: 6-2.

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:38.870
There it is! Nabae's got this!

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:41.330
Maruo, you're just getting started!

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:47.610
<i>Naïve... I was too naïve.</i>

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:51.740
<i>I don't even really understand the zone yet.</i>

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:55.160
<i>The fact that I thought I could
win if it worked is naïve!</i>

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:59.840
<i>I'm fighting right now to determine</i>

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:03.810
<i>whether I can go pro... whether I can 
really make a living playing tennis.</i>

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:07.920
<i>What good is it if I don't fight with 
everything I've learned so far?</i>

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:11.930
<i>It's time to fight.</i>

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:26.940
<i>Next is the final set.</i>

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:31.990
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:25.020
Next Time

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:02.470
I can't avoid more mistakes.

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:06.360
But I have to fight with 
everything I've learned so far.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:08.260
His aggressiveness is back.

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:11.090
Something unexpected is bound to 
happen from this turn of events.

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:12.290
I studied in Florida,

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:15.380
fortified my serve, trained my muscles...

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:20.140
I'm hitting this shot with nothing but 
changing the pace on my mind.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.250
Next time on <i>Baby Steps</i>,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:25.020
Final Set

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:23.520
"Final Set."

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:31.000
End
